.TH Y-NOT 1 "2026-10-24" "y-not 1.8.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
y-not \- explain why a user can or cannot access a file or directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B y-not
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I USER OPERATION PATH
.RB [ \-\-compare
.IR USER , USER ,... ]
.br
.B y-not
.RB { \-\-help | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B y-not
traces the access path to
.I PATH
and reports whether
.I USER
can perform
.I OPERATION
on it, and if not, which component of the path blocks access and why.

Instead of piecing together information from
.BR ls (1),
.BR namei (1),
.BR id (1),
and
.BR getfacl (1),
.B y-not
evaluates owner/group/other permissions, supplementary groups, POSIX ACLs,
Linux mount restrictions, immutable and append-only inode attributes, symlinks
and special files, and reports the result in
a single, human-readable explanation. Capability grants from pam_cap's
capability.conf are used as an approximation; this is not a simulation of an
actual target process's effective capabilities.
.PP
When missing group membership explains a denial, y-not names every group whose
mode bits or effective ACL permissions would grant the requested operation.
For ACLs, the effective permissions after the ACL mask are considered, so only
groups that would actually help are listed. In JSON these are reported as a
.B candidate_groups
array.
.PP
For a denied access, the text output summarizes the denial on labelled lines:
.B blocked at:
the first component that blocks the operation,
.B cause:
the rule in plain language,
.B proof:
the permission source and the raw/effective
.I rwx
bits it read, and
.B to allow:
the least-privilege change that would make the modeled verdict
.IR allowed :
a
.BR chmod (1)
bit, a group membership, an ACL grant or an ACL mask widening - or, for a
read-only or noexec mount, a note that the blocker is a mount option rather than
a permission. The suggestion is advisory; y-not never runs it. A fix that
changes a file the user does not own is prefixed with
.BR sudo (8),
since only the owner or root can apply it.
.PP
These human-readable labels map to the JSON
.BR reason ,
.B decision
and
.B remediation
fields respectively (the JSON field names are a stable contract and are not
renamed). When no permission change applies, such as a missing path, the
remediation is
.BR null .
.PP
The verdict above is the modeled one. When it can, y-not also asks the kernel
itself: it forks, drops to the target identity, and calls
.BR access (2)
on the resolved target, then reports whether the kernel agreed. The text output
gains one line:
.B verified:
when the kernel confirms the verdict,
.B MISMATCH:
when the kernel disagrees - most often a security module such as SELinux or
AppArmor denying an access the DAC/ACL model would grant - and
.B unverified:
with an honest reason when the probe could not run. In JSON this is an additive
top-level
.B verification
object whose
.B status
is
.IR confirmed ,
.I divergent
or
.IR not_probed ,
whose
.B kernel_allowed
is a boolean or
.B null
when not probed, and which carries a
.B detail
string. The cross-check never changes the verdict; it only corroborates or
flags it, so
.B schema_version
stays 1.
.SH OPERATION
.TP
.B read\fR, \fBr
Can the user read the file, or read directory entries to list their names?
.TP
.B write\fR, \fBw
Can the user write to the file, or create an entry in the directory? Creating an entry requires both write and traversal permission. Use the separate
.B delete
operation to check removal of a named entry.
.TP
.B execute\fR, \fBx
For a regular file, checks whether the user has permission to attempt execution;
y-not does not validate the executable format or check whether its interpreter
or loader is available. For a directory, checks search/traversal permission.
.TP
.B delete\fR, \fBd
Checks whether the named directory entry can be removed. Its parent directory
must grant write and search permission. In a sticky directory, only the entry
owner, directory owner or root can remove it. An immutable
.RB ( chattr
+i) or append-only
.RB ( chattr
+a) attribute on the entry or its parent also blocks removal, overriding
ownership. This does not check rename, removal of a non-empty directory or
process-specific security policy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a JSON document (schema_version 1) instead of text. Must be the first
argument. Always emits a valid document, even for an unknown user or a
path that could not be resolved.
.TP
.BI \-\-compare " USER,USER,..."
Also report which of the listed accounts are allowed the same
.I OPERATION
on the same
.IR PATH .
Must come last, after the three positional arguments. The primary verdict and
exit status are unchanged; the text output gains an
.B allowed for:
line and, with
.BR \-\-json ,
an additive top-level
.B allowed_for
array (present only when
.B \-\-compare
is given, so
.B schema_version
stays 1). The list is explicit: y-not never enumerates every account on the
system, because the name service may be incomplete and MAC confinement is
process-dependent. Duplicate and unknown names are skipped, and the primary
user is never compared against itself.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Access is allowed.
.TP
.B 1
Access is denied, or an error occurred (unknown user, invalid path, bad
arguments).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Check whether alice can read a file:
.B y-not alice read /srv/project/report.pdf
.TP
Same, using the short form:
.B y-not alice r /srv/project/report.pdf
.TP
Report which other accounts can read it:
.B y-not alice read /srv/project/report.pdf \-\-compare bob,backup
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B y-not
models Unix permissions and POSIX ACLs and applies read-only/noexec mount
restrictions. pam_cap grants are only an approximation of process capabilities;
SELinux and AppArmor do not change the allow/deny verdict: SELinux policy
evaluation and AppArmor's per-program confinement do not fit into a single
user/operation/path query. When the verdict is
.I allowed
and SELinux is enforcing or AppArmor has loaded profiles, y-not prints a
prominent warning that the kernel may still deny a confined process (and sets
.B may_override_verdict
in the JSON
.B mac
object). Read-only and noexec mount options are
included in the verdict; nosuid is informational. Mount options are read from
y-not's own mount namespace, which may differ from the target process's
namespace. Directory write checks model entry creation (write plus traversal),
not rename or removal of a non-empty directory. The
.B delete
operation checks named-entry removal and sticky-bit ownership. Immutable and
append-only inode attributes are read and override the verdict for
.B write
and
.BR delete ;
because append-only still permits appending, a
.B write
to an append-only file is reported as allowed and only
.B delete
is blocked. Attributes are known only when y-not can open the inode to read
them. y-not does not model process-specific security policy.
.PP
.B y-not
inspects each path component with the privileges of the user who runs it, not
those of the target user. If a parent directory denies the running process
search, y-not cannot inspect what lies beneath it; it then reports that the path
could not be inspected and leaves the verdict undetermined rather than guessing.
Run y-not as root, or as a user who can traverse the whole path, for a complete
diagnosis.
.PP
The
.B \-\-compare
option evaluates only the accounts named on the command line, each under the
same DAC/ACL model and caveats as the primary verdict. It is never a
system-wide answer to "who can access this": the name service may not expose
every account, and MAC confinement is process-dependent, so an exhaustive
enumeration could not be sound.
.PP
The kernel cross-check is a corroboration, never the verdict. y-not skips it
- reporting
.B not_probed
with the reason - whenever
.BR access (2)
would be blind to what actually drove the decision (an inode attribute, the
sticky rule) or whenever y-not could not fully resolve or traverse the path,
since a privileged probe could then succeed where y-not could not look and
manufacture a false divergence. The
.B delete
operation is never probed, because
.BR access (2)
cannot test entry removal. Probing another user's access requires root; run as
an unprivileged user, y-not can only cross-check its own identity and reports
.B requires root to verify as another user
otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stat (1),
.BR getfacl (1),
.BR namei (1),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR capability.conf (5)
